{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by constant warfare with Muslim forces. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab where he spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through a massive ransom payment. This long imprisonment shaped his worldview and made him one of the most experienced leaders in the Latin East while fostering a deep understanding of Islamic culture and politics.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond assumed power following the assassination of his father Raymond II and he immediately faced threats from the Nizari Ismailis known as the Assassins. His capture occurred during a campaign against Nur ad-Din and this event removed him from regional affairs for a decade. The experience taught him patience and diplomacy rather than reckless aggression since he witnessed the strength of Muslim unity firsthand. He returned to Tripoli with a mature perspective that often clashed with newer crusaders who arrived from Europe without knowledge of local complexities.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nHe acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule independently during certain periods of his reign. Raymond opposed the faction led by Guy of Lusignan and Sibylla because he believed their aggressive policies would destroy the kingdom. His political maneuvering aimed to preserve stability through treaties and alliances with Muslim neighbors like Saladin instead of pursuing constant military confrontation. Many nobles supported his cautious approach yet others viewed his closeness to Muslim leaders with suspicion and accused him of treason or excessive accommodation.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\nThe Battle of Hattin in 1187 proved disastrous for the Crusader states and Raymond survived the defeat while many others perished or were captured. He retreated to Tyre after the battle and died shortly thereafter under mysterious circumstances that some contemporaries attributed to poison or illness. His death left the kingdom without its most skilled diplomat at a time when strong leadership was essential for survival against Saladin's expanding empire. Historians often debate his legacy since some praise his realism and others criticize his reluctance to engage in holy war with full fervor. His life story illustrates the difficult balance between religious zeal and pragmatic statecraft in the medieval Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.00018771265}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.00044135057}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.016346596}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.02324752}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -4.172334e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.00018962035}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.0994463e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.017987669}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.029111486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.001955035}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.6934778}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Kingdom"